WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.190 --> 00:00:05.270
China has the most extensive network building
and deploying the fifth generation of cellular

00:00:05.270 --> 00:00:06.270
networks.

00:00:06.270 --> 00:00:09.639
5G runs up to 100 times faster than 4G.

00:00:09.639 --> 00:00:15.029
It has built 1.6 million 5G base stations
across the country, according to the state-run

00:00:15.029 --> 00:00:16.080
People’s Daily.

00:00:16.080 --> 00:00:19.320
In comparison, the U.S. has only about 100,000.

00:00:19.320 --> 00:00:24.600
Yet, instead of trailing China, the Americans
could have been the leader in 5G development

00:00:24.600 --> 00:00:27.470
had an immigrant been allowed to stay in the
country.

00:00:27.470 --> 00:00:29.770
Erdal Arikan may not be a household name.

00:00:29.770 --> 00:00:34.629
Yet he’s so extraordinary that Chinese telecom
giant Huawei rolled out the red carpet for

00:00:34.629 --> 00:00:37.460
him at its headquarters in Shenzhen in 2018.

00:00:37.460 --> 00:00:42.219
They treated him like a rock star because
he developed the fundamental technology for

00:00:42.219 --> 00:00:43.219
5G.

00:00:43.219 --> 00:00:47.519
Arikan was born and raised in Turkey, the
son of a doctor and a stay-at-home mom.

00:00:47.519 --> 00:00:52.860
He loved science and studied electrical engineering
at Middle East Technical University in Ankara.

00:00:52.860 --> 00:00:57.660
While in his first year, in 1977, Arikan’s
high test scores allowed him to transfer to

00:00:57.660 --> 00:01:02.569
the California Institute of Technology, known
as CalTech, one of the world’s top universities

00:01:02.569 --> 00:01:05.330
with a strong focus on science and engineering.

00:01:05.330 --> 00:01:10.010
While there, he took a particular interest
in information theory…a study of mathematical

00:01:10.010 --> 00:01:14.250
laws that govern communications, including
5G networks.

00:01:14.250 --> 00:01:19.340
Information theory was developed a few decades
earlier by the brilliant Claude Shannon at

00:01:19.340 --> 00:01:21.790
AT&amp;T’s research arm Bell Labs.

00:01:21.790 --> 00:01:25.290
His ideas laid the groundwork for the digital
revolution.

00:01:25.290 --> 00:01:28.510
He made the internet possible.

00:01:28.510 --> 00:01:32.960
In 1948, Shannon published a mathematical
theory that showed how to transmit messages

00:01:32.960 --> 00:01:35.790
accurately at the fastest speed possible.

00:01:35.790 --> 00:01:38.480
Say, Scott wants to send a message to Sarah.

00:01:38.480 --> 00:01:43.580
A transmitter encodes information into a signal,
which is corrupted by noise and then decoded

00:01:43.580 --> 00:01:44.750
by the receiver.

00:01:44.750 --> 00:01:48.910
Shannon’s theory showed how to calculate
the maximum rate at which messages can be

00:01:48.910 --> 00:01:55.830
sent with zero errors to ensure that the message
Scott sent is the same one Sarah received.

00:01:55.830 --> 00:01:59.990
According to Shannon, every communications
channel has a “speed limit” for transmitting

00:01:59.990 --> 00:02:03.460
information reliably, referred to as the “Shannon
limit”.

00:02:03.460 --> 00:02:08.190
However, Shannon did NOT provide a technique
for constructing the code to achieve the fastest

00:02:08.190 --> 00:02:11.270
rate possible or make it clear how to compile
such code.

00:02:11.270 --> 00:02:13.290
That’s where Arikan came in.

00:02:13.290 --> 00:02:17.590
After graduating from CalTech, he took his
interest in Shannon’s theory to MIT for

00:02:17.590 --> 00:02:18.980
his grad studies.

00:02:18.980 --> 00:02:23.310
His mentor there, Professor Robert Gallager
was a renowned pioneer in information theory

00:02:23.310 --> 00:02:26.600
who came close to the Shannon limit but never
reached it.

00:02:26.600 --> 00:02:30.700
Arikan would throw himself into this work
for the next twenty years, trying to attain

00:02:30.700 --> 00:02:32.880
what no one had yet been able to do.

00:02:32.880 --> 00:02:38.230
After finishing his doctoral thesis in 1986,
he briefly served as an assistant professor

00:02:38.230 --> 00:02:40.090
at the University of Illinois at Urbana-Champaign.

00:02:40.090 --> 00:02:46.360
However, he was reportedly unable to obtain
a permanent academic post in the U.S. or funding

00:02:46.360 --> 00:02:48.730
to continue his work on information theory.

00:02:48.730 --> 00:02:52.560
And as a foreign citizen, he had to find an
American employer interested in supporting

00:02:52.560 --> 00:02:54.959
him in order to remain in the country.

00:02:54.959 --> 00:02:59.620
But he failed to do so…and had no choice
but to return to Turkey.

00:02:59.620 --> 00:03:03.760
He once told Wired the reason scientists and
engineers in the U.S. don’t spend decades

00:03:03.760 --> 00:03:08.181
working on the same problem is “Because
they wouldn't be able to get tenure; they

00:03:08.181 --> 00:03:11.030
wouldn't be able to get research funding.”

00:03:11.030 --> 00:03:15.590
He returned to Turkey to join the country’s
first private, non-profit research institution,

00:03:15.590 --> 00:03:19.330
Bilkent University - now a leading institution
in the country.

00:03:19.330 --> 00:03:22.890
He established its engineering school and
gave lectures and published papers.

00:03:22.890 --> 00:03:26.069
But the Shannon limit kept nagging at him.

00:03:26.069 --> 00:03:31.209
How do you transmit messages accurately at
the fastest speed possible?

00:03:31.209 --> 00:03:34.849
Messages that you send - letters and emojis
- are converted into a string of ones and

00:03:34.849 --> 00:03:36.560
zeroes called bits.

00:03:36.560 --> 00:03:40.800
The problem is noise can distort the message,
so redundancies are required to make sure

00:03:40.800 --> 00:03:43.400
the correct message is received.

00:03:43.400 --> 00:03:46.910
Redundancies ensure that if an error does
creep in, you can still be sure of the correct

00:03:46.910 --> 00:03:49.870
string and what the original message intended
to say.

00:03:49.870 --> 00:03:54.850
However, there is a trade-off: the more redundancies
you add the slower the transmission rate.

00:03:54.850 --> 00:03:56.940
So, how did you get around that problem?

00:03:56.940 --> 00:04:02.360
After working on it for over two decades,
Arikan had a EUREKA moment in late 2005.

00:04:02.360 --> 00:04:07.381
His solution was to make a copy of the channel
and transfer the noise from one channel to

00:04:07.381 --> 00:04:11.920
the other, so you’re left with one noisy
channel, and the other that’s much cleaner.

00:04:11.920 --> 00:04:15.239
The noise-free channel could in theory reach
the Shannon limit.

00:04:15.239 --> 00:04:18.160
He dubbed his discovery: “polar codes”

00:04:18.160 --> 00:04:22.639
In reference to banishing noise to the North
Pole, so that there is clear communication

00:04:22.639 --> 00:04:23.910
at the South Pole.

00:04:23.910 --> 00:04:28.460
He continued to tinker with his discovery,
and in 2009, he published his findings in

00:04:28.460 --> 00:04:31.500
the field’s top scientific journal, to much
fanfare.

00:04:31.500 --> 00:04:37.380
Arikan tried to sell the idea of polar codes
to both Qualcomm and Seagate, but they declined.

00:04:37.380 --> 00:04:40.550
No American company was interested in his
ideas!

00:04:40.550 --> 00:04:44.280
He blamed himself for not being a good enough
salesperson, telling Wired:

00:04:44.280 --> 00:04:47.410
“I was an academic who did not know how
to promote an idea.

00:04:47.410 --> 00:04:50.260
Perhaps I did not believe in the idea that
strongly myself.”

00:04:50.260 --> 00:04:54.900
But one company did believe in the idea very
strongly.

00:04:54.900 --> 00:04:58.990
The head honchos at Huawei saw the potential
of polar codes.

00:04:58.990 --> 00:05:04.160
They believed it was the breakthrough needed
to leap from 4G networks to 5G.

00:05:04.160 --> 00:05:09.479
Polar codes are considered a direct challenge
to the American-developed coding method "LDPC"

00:05:09.479 --> 00:05:11.730
and are believed to be superior to it.

00:05:11.730 --> 00:05:16.630
The Chinese company bet big on polar codes
– investing hundreds of millions of dollars

00:05:16.630 --> 00:05:18.240
to build on Arikan’s work.

00:05:18.240 --> 00:05:23.360
In fact, when Canadian tech giant Nortel went
under, Huawei poached its top researcher who

00:05:23.360 --> 00:05:24.910
grew up in China.

00:05:24.910 --> 00:05:28.270
Wen Tong would head Huawei’s R&amp;D division
on 5G.

00:05:28.270 --> 00:05:33.250
Huawei’s engineers came up with the software
to make polar codes work in practice and invented

00:05:33.250 --> 00:05:35.200
some of the first 5G technologies.

00:05:35.200 --> 00:05:40.120
Today, Huawei holds the majority of polar
code patents for 5G and has positioned itself

00:05:40.120 --> 00:05:42.820
as a world leader in tech innovation.

00:05:42.820 --> 00:05:47.870
Western countries insist Huawei’s technology
may intentionally contain security holes that

00:05:47.870 --> 00:05:52.810
could be used by the Chinese government for
spying…which Huawei flatly denies.

00:05:52.810 --> 00:05:55.729
The U.S. has banned its equipment.

00:05:55.729 --> 00:06:00.110
Despite the tension between the east and the
west over the development of 5G technology,

00:06:00.110 --> 00:06:05.770
Arikan is thankful that his tireless work
and refusal to give up…has finally paid

00:06:05.770 --> 00:06:06.770
off.

00:06:06.770 --> 00:06:14.780
As engineers, there is no greater reward than
seeing our ideas turn into reality.

00:06:14.780 --> 00:06:16.470
Thank you for making it happen.

00:06:16.470 --> 00:06:19.250
Engineers like Arikan are shaping the world
of tomorrow.

